main='''<main id="main"><div class="atlas-breadcrumb"><a href="/">Overview</a><span>/</span><a href="/evidence/">Evidence</a><span>/</span><span>Signed reconstruction</span></div><div class="page-intro"><div class="eyebrow">21 October 2026 · Reconstruction contract 0.1</div><h1>Signed traces survive.<br>The phase gate fails.</h1><p class="lede">A frozen reconstruction contract separates fast carrier, complex rotating envelope and squared-voltage proxy across 891 circuit channel-records.</p></div><aside class="callout"><strong>0 / 891 composite gates passed · Action-anchor HOLD · Physical promotion 0</strong><p>Constructor verification only. No interior point meets the conservative uniform phase floor. No native clock, action ratio or physical correspondence is inferred. EXIT-03 remains HOLD.</p></aside><div class="actions"><a class="button secondary" href="/sources/signed-reconstruction-v0.1/REPORT.md">Read full report ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="/sources/signed-reconstruction-v0.1/CONTRACT.json">Inspect frozen contract</a></div><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">01</div><div><h2>Retain complex phase before squaring.</h2><p>Each channel’s fixed carrier comes from its previously explored record zero. Records 1–99 are evaluated without carrier retuning, using a ±5 kHz positive-frequency band. The records vary in drive-frequency metadata; they are not identical repeated trials, and the extension is not blinded or independently preregistered.</p><p>The signed finite-DFT analytic representation round-trips all evaluated records with maximum absolute error 1.5544×10⁻¹⁵ V. Demodulation retains the complex envelope. Taking its magnitude or square discards sign and phase.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://doi.org/10.11588/DATA/6MEKHI">Published source dataset →</a></div></section><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">02</div><div><h2>The failed threshold stays frozen.</h2><p>The gate requires signed RMS residual ≤5% and phase-floor coverage ≥95%. Every record fails the composite test; all interior phase fractions are zero. Median RMS residuals range from 4.26% to 9.86% by channel.</p><p>The phase floor uses the maximum complex residual over the entire post-trigger window. Its conservatism can retain endpoint effects despite an interior guard. This run does not identify which contribution dominates, and the gate is not loosened after observing failure.</p><a class="text-link" href="/sources/signed-reconstruction-v0.1/RESULTS.json">Machine-readable summary →</a><a class="text-link" href="/sources/signed-reconstruction-v0.1/RECORDS.csv">All 891 record diagnostics ↓</a></div></section><section class="chapter"><div class="chapter-label">03</div><div><h2>A numerical residual has a defined reference.</h2><p>The report gives explicit amplitude, phase and squared-envelope truncation inequalities relative to the full finite-DFT analytic signal. These bounds do not include instrument gain, timing skew, nonperiodic boundary error or a complete physical energy map.</p><p>Five synthetic controls pass. Amplitude and squared-envelope inequalities are checked on all 891 records; empirical accepted-point phase checks have no eligible points. Independent review is pending. Development — no registry entry.</p><p>Next: freeze a boundary-treatment comparison against synthetic transients and nonperiodic carriers, then distinguish endpoint effects from omitted bands. Absolute native support and energy calibration remain open.</p><a class="text-link" href="/sources/signed-reconstruction-v0.1/reconstruct.py">Reproduction code ↓</a><a class="text-link" href="/evidence/energy-clock/">Energy-clock-support theorem →</a></div></section></main>'''
